Sundog Stride
Ingredients: COOL & WEST COAST JAZZ + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Tuba
Creative Catalyst: Musical horns forged from fallen nickel-iron meteorite fragments and regulated by antique Swiss clockwork escapements.

Sundog Stride grafts the relaxed, breezy linear counterpoint of mid-century West Coast cool jazz onto an intensely joyful, high-speed bounce driven by an agile, dancing tuba. Instead of laid-back melancholy, the genre erupts in sun-drenched euphoria: intricate counter-melodies between flugelhorn and baritone sax flutter over relentless, buoyant tuba basslines, with the tactile mechanical click of exposed clockwork escapements providing an uncanny, metronomic pocket.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 145–185 BPM. A breathless, buoyant 4/4 swing feel characterized by walking staccato tuba lines, crisp ride-cymbal pings, and interlocking clockwork clicks that create a buoyant, forward-rolling momentum.
- Harmonic Palette: Sparkling Major 7th, 9th, and 11th chords built around the Lydian mode; sunny, open brass voicings that borrow the polyphonic arrangement smarts of Gerry Mulligan and Dave Brubeck, completely purged of blues sorrow and reframed in radiant daylight.
- Production Signatures: Close-mic valve bleed (capturing the clatter and metallic rattle of mechanical gears), dry coastal studio acoustics with minimal chamber delay, and extreme left-right stereo separation of counterpoint brass to showcase interwoven improvisations.
- Dynamics: Songs typically launch with a solo escapement ticking and a soft, rhythmic tuba puff before detonating into an exuberant, multi-horn celebration; arrangements breathe through gossamer vibraphone interludes before hurtling back into roaring, collective joy.

Scene & Origin Lore
Sundog Stride caught fire along the sun-bleached boardwalks of Hermosa Beach and Santa Cruz in the mid-1960s. The spark belonged to Tobias Finch, an eccentric horologist and brass repairman who recovered remnants of a nickel-iron Pallasite meteorite from a coastal crater. Finch hammered the celestial alloy into custom flared bells and substituted sluggish tuba piston valves with salvaged 18th-century Swiss pocket-watch gears. The resulting instruments weighed less, resonated with an uncanny bell-like brightness, and allowed tubists to play lightning-fast bebop runs with frictionless ease. The scene’s devotees—dubbed "Chronos"—wore loose sand-colored linen suits, rolled cuffs, and mirrored spectacles, gathering at beachfront pavilions at high noon to dance a frantic, grinning hop while synchronized antique pocket watches pinned to their lapels glinted in the Pacific glare.

Typical Instruments: Meteorite-alloy CC tuba (fitted with clockwork escapement valve assemblies), baritone saxophone, flugelhorn, vibraphone with nickel tines, brushed maple snare drum, wooden gear clickers, and upright bass.

For Fans Of: Gerry Mulligan Quartet, Sons of Kemet, Vince Guaraldi Trio, The Canadian Brass.

